{"“A \003quarter to six\004,” answered the midwife.\n“And what if I really am dying?” thought Olga Mihalovna, looking at her husband’s head and the window-panes on which the rain was beating.","The Party","Anton Pavlovich Chekhov",true},
{"At \0035:45\004 the following morning, the General was standing outside George’s tent in full dress uniform, carrying a black leather attaché case.","Paths of Glory","Jeffrey Archer",true},
{"At \0035:45\004 a power-transformer on a pole beside the abandoned Tracker Brothers’ Truck Depot exploded in a flash of purple light, spraying twisted chunks of metal onto the shingled roof.","It","Stephen King",true},
},
["05:46"]={
{"Herbert could feel nothing. He wrote a legal-sounding phrase to the effect that the sentence had been carried out at \0035:46 am\004, adding, ‘without a snag’. The burial party had cursed him quietly as they’d hacked at the thick roots and tight soil.","A Whispered Name","William Brodrick",true},
{"It was a fine summer morning. The hands of the Dutch clock in the hall pointed to thirteen minutes past nine; those of the ormolu clock in the sitting-room to eleven minutes past ten; those of the carriage clock on the bookshelf to \003fourteen minutes to six\004. In other words, it was exactly eight; and Mrs. Hignett acknowledged the fact by moving her head on the pillow, opening her eyes, and sitting up in bed. She always woke at eight precisely.","Three Men and a Maid","P.G. Wodehouse",true},
},
["05:47"]={
{"His eyelids pried open. The bedside lamp was lit. Rue hovered over him, flicking her finger at his earlobe. What the hell? The clock said it was \003forty-seven minutes past five\004. Before dawn. On a Sunday.","Christmas Coverup","Cassie Miles",false},
},
["05:48"]={
{"A Bacardi mirror with fogged glass hangs above the backbar next to a St. Pauli Girl clock, the clock’s hands frozen at \003twelve minutes to six\004.","Northwest Corner","John Burnham Schwartz",true},
},
["05:49"]={
{"Towards morning he slept more deeply. Even so, the harsh incessant ring of the telephone woke him to instant consciousness. The illuminated dial of his travelling clock showed \0035:49 a.m.\004","Shroud for a Nightingale","P.D. James",true},
{"It was \003eleven minutes to six\004. After a moment he idly drew the center drawer of the desk out over his lap. For a moment he stared at the gun without recognition. Then he gave a yelp and leaped up. She had put it back!","The Comforts of Home","Flannery O’Connor",true},
},
["05:50"]={
{"It was was \0035:50\004 when he staggered from the room, heading he knew not where or for how long, but hoping only that whoever had been guiding him lately would not desert him now.","The Princess Bride","William Goldman",true},
},
["05:51"]={
{"It was \003nine minutes to six o’clock\004 and an hour before dawn. The brigade was not due to move today, and so, just as they did every other day, along with every British corps in the field, the four battalions stood to arms in case of attack. Each company paraded and then marched to form with its battalion at its alarm post.","Whose Business is to Die","Adrian Goldsworthy",true},
},
["05:52"]={
{"At \0035:52 am\004 paramedics from the St. Petersburg Fire Department and SunStar Medic One ambulance service responded to a medical emergency call at 12201 Ninth Street North, St. Petersburg, apartment 2210.","Silent Witness","Mark Fuhrman",true},
},
["05:53"]={
{"The day had been specially chosen because the meteorologists had assured them that the sun would rise at \003five fifty-three\004 and there would be no rain. The meteorologists would prove to be right about the sun, but as if to show their fallibility at four-eleven a steady drizzle began.","As the Crow Flies","Jeffrey Archer",true},
{"At \003five fifty-three\004, as a blood-red sun peeped over the horizon, a Verey pistol was fired and Charlie looked back to see the sky lit up behind him.","As the Crow Flies","Jeffrey Archer",true},
{"The gate log showed he’d signed out at \003seven minutes to six\004 that morning, slightly earlier than usual. Because they were conscientious employees, the spaceport immediately called the police and sent someone around to Gemma Tivon.","Fallen Dragon","Peter F. Hamilton",true},
},
["05:54"]={
{"This morning, the sun came up at \003six minutes to six\004. There was already heavy early-morning rush-hour traffic on the Majesta Bridge. Meyer was driving. Carella was riding shotgun.","Merely Hate","Ed McBain",true},
},
["05:55"]={
{"One, two, three, four, five, six… She wound each clock in turn, three turns each, putting just enough power in the springs to get them ticking in time for Mr. Westcott’s inspection. Speaking of which…it was \003five minutes to six\004. Five minutes before Mr. Westcott would arrive to inspect his clocks. Helena swallowed. She could hear footsteps thundering in the room below and the occasional exclamation from Stanley.","The House of One Hundred Clocks","A.M. Howell",true},
{"Billy doesn’t drive to the parking garage. The parking garage is done. At \003five to six\004 he parks on Main Street a few blocks from the Gerard Tower. Plenty of curbside spaces at this hour and the sidewalk is deserted.","Billy Summers","Stephen King",true},
{"It was \0035:55 am\004 and raining hard when I pedalled up to the bike stand just outside the forecourt of the station and dashed inside. I raced past the bookstall, where all the placards of the Yorkshire Post (a morning paper) read ‘York Horror’, but also ‘Terrific February Gales at Coast’.","The Lost Luggage Porter","Andrew Martin",true},
},
["05:56"]={
{"ANNA PETRESCU TOUCHED the button on the top of her bedside clock. It glowed \0035:56 A.M.\004 Another four minutes and it would have woken her with the early morning news. But not today.","False Impression","Jeffrey Archer",true},
{"The officer with the chipped nose went over to borrow the watch of General Feraud. They bent their heads over them for a time. “That’s it. At \003four minutes to six\004 by yours.”","A Set of Six","Joseph Conrad",true},
},
["05:57"]={
{"She smiles a bit but the next breath comes out louder like a moan. At \00305:57\004 I say, “Ma, it’s nearly six,” so she gets up to make dinner but she doesn’t eat any.","Room","Emma Donoghue",true},
},
["05:58"]={
{"Loud crashes came from the flat below at \0035:58\004 this morning, though she’d been clock-watching long before that.","The Woman Downstairs","Elisabeth Carpenter",true},
{"Annika Giannini woke with a start. She saw that it was \0035:58 a.m.\004","The Girl Who Kicked the Hornets’ Nest","Stieg Larsson",true},
{"Grant stepped into his office at St. Mary’s Lutheran Church \003just before six\004 the next morning. His unusually early arrival – skipping his regular morning exercises – was spurred by a desire to pull up the essay that he had written years earlier, and to start the process of updating it, as requested.","Under the Golden Dome","Ray Keating",true},
},
["05:59"]={
{"At \003one minute to six\004 Hollis pulled out his watch. He sighed, replaced the time-piece, and leaned back in his chair. A glance out through the window showed him that the street was deserted except for here and there a cow pony drooping over one of the hitching rails and a wagon or two standing in front of a store.","The Coming of the Law","Charles Alden Seltzer",true},
{"The shades in the corner room on the southeast were the first to be raised. It was the old rector’s bedroom. He was reading his Bible \003before the clock had struck six\004.","The Hammer of God","Bo Giertz",true},
{"It must have been \003dawn\004 when Don Quixote left the inn, so happy, so gallant, so overjoyed at having been knighted that his joy was bursting through his horse’s girths.","Don Quixote","Miguel de Cervantes",true},
},
